le cx="12" cy="12" r="4"></circle><path d="M12 2v2"></path><path d="M12 20v2"></path><path d="m4.93 4.93 1.41 1.41"></path><path d="m17.66 17.66 1.41 1.41"></path><path d="M2 12h2"></path><path d="M20 12h2"></path><path d="m6.34 17.66-1.41 1.41"></path><path d="m19.07 4.93-1.41 1.41"></path></svg><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-moon-star hidden text-text dark:block" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 5h4"></path><path d="M20 3v4"></path><path d="M20.985 12.486a9 9 0 1 1-9.473-9.472c.405-.022.617.46.402.803a6 6 0 0 0 8.268 8.268c.344-.215.825-.004.803.401"></path></svg></div></div></div></button><div class="flex items-center"><a href="https://twitter.com/askalphaxiv" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Twitter" class="flex shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full p-3 text-text" data-state="closed"><div class="interactable-overlay bg-overlay"></div><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="currentColor"><path d="M23.643 4.937c-.835.37-1.732.62-2.675.733.962-.576 1.7-1.49 2.048-2.578-.9.534-1.897.922-2.958 1.13-.85-.904-2.06-1.47-3.4-1.47-2.572 0-4.658 2.086-4.658 4.66 0 .364.042.718.12 1.06-3.873-.195-7.304-2.05-9.602-4.868-.4.69-.63 1.49-.63 2.342 0 1.616.823 3.043 2.072 3.878-.764-.025-1.482-.234-2.11-.583v.06c0 2.257 1.605 4.14 3.737 4.568-.392.106-.803.162-1.227.162-.3 0-.593-.028-.877-.082.593 1.85 2.313 3.198 4.352 3.234-1.595 1.25-3.604 1.995-5.786 1.995-.376 0-.747-.022-1.112-.065 2.062 1.323 4.51 2.093 7.14 2.093 8.57 0 13.255-7.098 13.255-13.254 0-.2-.005-.402-.014-.602.91-.658 1.7-1.477 2.323-2.41z"></path></svg></a></div><div class="invisible flex overflow-hidden transition-opacity duration-200 lg:visible pointer-events-none opacity-0"><a class="w-fit p-3 text-xs whitespace-nowrap text-subtext hover:underline" href="/blog">Blog</a><a href="https://github.com/alphaxiv/feedback/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="w-fit p-3 text-xs whitespace-nowrap text-subtext hover:underline">Send Feedback?</a></div></div></nav></div><main class="min-h-0 w-full flex-auto flex-col [--audio-player-room:0px] has-[[data-audio-player]]:[--audio-player-room:4rem] sm:w-0 pr-(--workspace-room-right) pl-(--workspace-room-left)"><!--$--><div class="relative h-dvh"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"ScholarlyArticle","headline":"A Society of AI Scientists","abstract":"Artificial intelligence agents can now conduct end-to-end research, yet whether independent agents can collaborate, critique peer findings and build cumulative knowledge as a scientific community remains unclear. We present an exploratory twelve-week study documenting the activity of 936 autonomous agents across 35 human-operated laboratories interacting in a shared preprint server and messaging workspace. During this time, agents produced 936 studies along with their corresponding code and data artifacts. They also exchanged 9,500 messages distributed across 229 agent-created chat rooms. As participation grew from a single laboratory, expanding into a community of hundreds of agents, distinct research cultures and norms emerged throughout. For example, peers formed topical hubs where it was common to publicly retract mistaken claims before publication as well as acknowledge negative findings as often as positive ones. As publication volume accelerated, agents in one laboratory also founded the Journal of Agent Research, an agent-run peer-review venue whose editors inspected code and provided scrutiny over experiment results. Laboratories in this study focused on topics ranging from language model finetuning to agent-metascience studies investigating how agents interpret data and report findings. Additionally, agent-run reproducibility audits confirmed that reported findings were consistent with the output of the agents experiments. And while blinded evaluations against recent human preprints rated agent studies higher on experimental rigor and measured claims, human preprints were rated higher on novelty and significance. To go along with this paper, we release the full twelve-week archive of manuscripts, code, data, peer reviews and chat transcripts. Together, these observations document how scientific norms, structure, and failure modes emerge in large-scale societies of agents.","author":[{"@type":"Person","name":"Rehaan Ahmad","url":"https://www.alphaxiv.org/@rehaan-ahmad","affiliation":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Undergraduate, Stanford University"},"sameAs":["https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=-bA8eT4AAAAJ","https://www.linkedin.com/in/ahmadrehaan"]},{"@type":"Person","name":"Myles Anderson","url":"https://www.alphaxiv.org/@myles-anderson","sameAs":["https://www.linkedin.com/in/myles-anderson/","https://github.com/myles332","https://x.com/nmylesanderson"]},{"@type":"Person","name":"Ao Qu","url":"https://www.alphaxiv.org/@ao-qu","affiliation":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Massachusetts Institute of Technology"},"sameAs":["https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=qKdzvdwAAAAJ","https://quao627.github.io/"]},{"@type":"Person","name":"Mars Gao","url":"https://www.alphaxiv.org/@liyao-mars-gao","affiliation":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Stanford University"},"sameAs":["https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=JnmjCO4AAAAJ","https://marsgaocv.github.io/","https://github.com/gaoliyao"]},{"@type":"Person","name":"Yubin Kim","url":"https://www.alphaxiv.org/@yubin-kim","affiliation":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Google DeepMind, MIT"},"sameAs":["https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=tYK2WmQAAAAJ","https://ybkim95.github.io/","https://www.linkedin.com/in/yubinkim95","https://github.com/ybkim95","https://twitter.com/ybkim95_ai"]},{"@type":"Person","name":"Josiah Aklilu","url":"https://www.alphaxiv.org/@josiah-aklilu"